body.append(P('Stocks slipped Friday as Nvidia&rsquo;s leadership turmoil after Jensen Huang&rsquo;s exit kept pressure on chipmakers, and a hotter-than-hoped 6.5% yearly rise in wholesale prices left traders all but certain the Fed will raise rates at its Dec. 9 meeting; the Dow still edged up, and all three indexes posted their best week since early August. || Next week brings Home Depot and Nvidia results on Tuesday, Target and Lowe&rsquo;s on Wednesday, Walmart on Thursday, plus remarks Monday from Fed Chair Kevin Warsh.'))
body.append('<h3>What analysts are saying</h3>')
body.append('<ul class="picks">\n<li><b>Analysts leaned toward buying: PANW</b> (Palo Alto Networks, a cybersecurity software maker). KeyBanc raised the stock to overweight from sector weight, betting company security budgets will speed up through 2027 and calling the recent dip a buying chance. Risk: the shares gave back an early gain to close about flat, and budgets could grow more slowly than KeyBanc expects.</li>\n<li><b>Analysts leaned toward buying: DPZ</b> (Domino’s Pizza, the delivery and carryout pizza chain). Baird upgraded the shares to outperform from neutral, seeing a clearer path to better same-store sales and fatter franchisee profits. Risk: a stretched consumer facing higher interest rates could cut back on takeout.</li>\n<li><b>Analysts leaned toward buying: ECL</b> (Ecolab, which sells cleaning, sanitation and water-treatment products to businesses). Wells Fargo raised it to overweight from equal weight, expecting the company’s pricing surcharge to stick. Risk: customers could push back on the extra charges if costs ease.</li>\n<li><b>Analysts turned cautious: LOW</b> (Lowe’s, the home-improvement retailer). Stifel cut the stock to sell from hold, with analyst Andrew Carter pointing to a weaker housing market, margin pressure ahead and soft do-it-yourself demand. Risk to the bearish view: Lowe’s reports Wednesday, and a better-than-feared quarter could lift the shares.</li>\n<li><b>Analysts turned cautious: AVGO</b> (Broadcom, a maker of networking and custom AI chips). Rothschild &amp; Co Redburn lowered it to neutral from buy, seeing limited near-term upside and concerns about Google’s in-house custom chip tools. Risk to that view: Wolfe Research reiterated its outperform rating the same day, citing strong AI demand.</li>\n</ul><p class="note">This is information, not advice.</p>')
