body.append('<h3>Deer outlook: Good</h3>')
body.append(P("Opening morning of modern gun season comes with nearly everything in the hunter's favor: the rut is at or near its peak in north Arkansas, the dawn is below freezing, the barometer has climbed behind the front, and the wind is light. Best windows: from about 6:08 a.m., a half hour before sunrise, until about 8:40; again from 10 a.m. to 2 p.m., when rutting bucks cruise in daylight looking for does; and from 2:55 p.m. to the 4:55 sunset.||<b>Today's tip:</b> on a still, frosty dawn your scent sinks and creeps downhill, pooling in the hollows and creek bottoms, so sit above the trails you are watching rather than below them; once the sun warms the slopes by mid-morning the air turns and rises. With more than 300,000 hunters expected afield this weekend, wear the required blaze orange from the truck to the stand, and know what lies beyond your target before you squeeze.||<b>Tomorrow, Sunday (estimate: Good).</b> Another cold, near-calm dawn and steady high pressure, with the rut still in full swing; the same windows apply, from 6:09 a.m., 10 to 2, and 2:55 to sunset. <b>Tomorrow's tip:</b> after a day of opening-weekend gunfire, older deer slip into the thickest cover they know, cutovers, briar patches and creek-bottom cane, and bucks trailing does keep moving through midday; a patient hunter on the edge of that cover, staying put through lunch, often does better on Sunday than on Saturday."))
body.append('<h3>Fishing outlook: Fair</h3>')
body.append(P("Lake water is cooling fast after the frosty nights, and the bite is slow early, but the light breeze and a sunny afternoon near 53 should stir the fish in the warmer hours. Best times: late morning through mid-afternoon as the water warms, and the last hour before the 4:55 sunset.||<b>Today's tip:</b> at Craighead Forest Lake and Lake Frierson, crappie are drawing together on brush piles and around fishing piers and docks, often 8 to 15 feet down; fish a small minnow or a 1/16-ounce jig slowly, just above the cover. Bass will move to the sunny north banks by afternoon, where rocks and wood soak up warmth; a slow-rolled crankbait or a jig dragged along the bottom beats fast, flashy lures in cold water.||<b>Tomorrow, Sunday (estimate: Fair).</b> Another frosty start and light wind, with a slightly warmer afternoon near 57; the best hours again run from late morning to mid-afternoon and the last hour of light. <b>Tomorrow's tip:</b> catfish feed through the cool months in the deeper water near the dam and old creek channels; a piece of cut shad or chicken liver on the bottom, fished patiently through the warm part of the afternoon, is the surest bet for a Sunday stringer."))
body.append('<h3>In season now, and what is next (zones 3, 4, 6 and 8)</h3>')
